"""Private Eyes editorial content. Keep claims tied to the released app."""
from html import escape

GUIDES = [
('hide-or-encrypt-photos-android', 'Hiding vs encrypting photos on Android', 'Understand what hiding, locking and encrypting photos each protect, and choose a workflow that fits your needs.', '''
<p>Hiding a photo changes where you see it. Encryption changes how its stored bytes can be read. Before moving personal files into any app, check both its access controls and what happens to the original.</p>
<h2>Three different jobs</h2><ul><li><strong>Organization:</strong> moving an image out of the main gallery can reduce accidental exposure while browsing.</li><li><strong>Access control:</strong> a lock screen asks for authentication before displaying files.</li><li><strong>Encrypted storage:</strong> protected file contents require the appropriate decryption key to read.</li></ul>
<p>These features can coexist. A hidden album is not automatically insecure, and a screen labelled “vault” is not evidence of encryption. Read the specific product’s documentation rather than judging its icon.</p>
<h2>What Private Eyes does</h2><p>Private Eyes encrypts imported file contents, filenames and stored thumbnails on your Android phone. It uses a pattern and Android device authentication to open its device-bound vault. The app has no Internet permission, account, analytics or cloud storage.</p>
<p>It preserves imported original bytes. Importing creates a protected copy; it does not automatically protect the source in Gallery, a messaging app or a cloud account. Review the import results and choose whether to remove originals separately.</p>
<h2>Choose based on your situation</h2><p>For a tidier gallery, organization may be sufficient. For private files stored on a shared or lost phone, consider encryption, automatic locking, screenshot controls and the strength of your device lock. For irreplaceable files, also plan recovery: Private Eyes currently has no backup or recovery service.</p>
<h2>Try the workflow with an ordinary photo first</h2><ol><li>Import a harmless sample and open the vault copy.</li><li>Lock the app and confirm that reopening requires authentication.</li><li>Review where the original remains.</li><li>Test exporting a copy before relying on the workflow.</li></ol><p>A compromised operating system or an already-unlocked session remains outside the protection an app can promise. We do not claim forensic invisibility or independent security certification.</p>'''),
('remove-originals-after-vault-import', 'Remove original photos after importing into a vault', 'Check successful imports, review remaining copies and understand Android’s source deletion confirmation.', '''
<p>Importing a photo into Private Eyes creates an encrypted vault copy. The original can still be visible in Gallery or Files. Remove that source only after you have checked the import and decided how you will preserve anything irreplaceable.</p>
<h2>Use the import result</h2><ol><li>Unlock Private Eyes and choose Import. Select the photos, videos or documents you want.</li><li>Read the result: imported, failed and not processed files are reported separately. Retry unfinished files when needed.</li><li>Open important vault copies to check that they are usable. Remember that an unsupported preview does not necessarily mean the original failed to import.</li><li>At the import result, choose <strong>Remove originals…</strong> if you want to remove the successfully imported sources. Review Android’s confirmation before accepting.</li></ol>
<p>Only successful imports are offered for removal. Some file providers cannot grant deletion access or support bulk removal. In those cases, Private Eyes explains that you need to manage the source in Gallery or Files. Choosing Done leaves originals in place.</p>
<h2>A source is not every copy</h2><p>A photo may also exist in a cloud library, a message attachment, another device, a download or a trash folder. Private Eyes cannot enumerate or erase all those copies. Deleting a local source does not prove that a cloud copy has disappeared.</p>
<p>Google Photos distinguishes deleting an item from removing it from a device, and deletion can affect other devices with backup enabled. Review <a href="https://support.google.com/photos/answer/6128858?hl=en">Google’s current deletion guidance</a> before changing a synced library. Do not treat cloud deletion as interchangeable with local cleanup.</p>
<h2>Keep the tradeoff visible</h2><p>Private Eyes currently has no encrypted backup or recovery service. If you remove the only other copy and later lose this phone, its protected keys or your pattern, your file may be permanently lost. Keep irreplaceable material somewhere you trust, understanding that exported copies are outside vault protection.</p>
<p>Deletion also does not guarantee physical erasure from flash storage. For future captures, the private camera avoids creating a Gallery original in the first place.</p>'''),
('private-camera-android', 'Take private photos without adding them to Gallery', 'Use the Private Eyes camera to capture directly into an offline encrypted vault and understand its limits.', '''
<p>The Private Eyes camera captures directly into the vault instead of publishing a new photo to Gallery. This avoids the extra step of importing a camera-roll image and deciding whether to delete its original.</p>
<h2>Capture directly into the vault</h2><ol><li>Unlock Private Eyes using the device verification and vault pattern.</li><li>Open Camera and grant camera access if Android asks.</li><li>Frame the shot. Use supported zoom and tap-to-focus controls as needed.</li><li>Take the photo, then review it inside the vault.</li><li>Lock the vault when you finish.</li></ol>
<p>New photos and videos captured by the private camera have identifying metadata removed before encryption. Imported files follow a different rule: their original bytes are preserved, including metadata they already contain.</p>
<h2>Photos and short silent video</h2><p>The current camera supports photos and short silent videos. Video recording is limited to two minutes and 120 MiB, and available memory can reduce the limit or prevent recording. Camera modes and quality choices depend on the device. This is not an unlimited video recorder.</p>
<h2>Sharing creates a separate copy</h2><p>Exporting a file produces an unencrypted copy outside the vault. For supported JPEG photos, the cleaned export option removes embedded metadata and re-encodes the image; the stored original remains unchanged. Check the exported result before sending it.</p>
<p>Metadata removal cannot hide information visible in the picture itself. Faces, addresses on paper, reflections and recognizable surroundings can still disclose information.</p>
<h2>Before you rely on the camera</h2><p>Try a harmless capture on your phone and confirm the result opens. Keep sufficient free space. Private Eyes has no cloud backup or recovery service, so direct capture makes a preservation plan especially important for irreplaceable photos.</p>'''),
('lost-phone-vault-recovery', 'What happens to a photo vault when you lose your phone?', 'Understand Private Eyes device-bound storage, pattern loss and the limits of recovery before storing irreplaceable files.', '''
<p><strong>Private Eyes currently cannot recover a vault from a lost phone.</strong> It stores encrypted files locally and uses device-bound key protection. There is no account, recovery phrase, cloud backup or developer-held recovery key.</p>
<h2>Different problems, different outcomes</h2><ul><li><strong>Lost or broken phone:</strong> a replacement phone does not automatically receive a usable Private Eyes vault.</li><li><strong>Forgotten pattern:</strong> there is no reset service that restores access to the encrypted contents.</li><li><strong>Uninstalling or clearing app data:</strong> this can permanently remove the local vault and access material.</li><li><strong>Removing or forcibly resetting the screen lock:</strong> this can invalidate the device key and make the vault inaccessible.</li></ul>
<h2>Plan before removing your originals</h2><p>Keep a separate copy of irreplaceable material in a location you trust. Private Eyes can export readable copies, but those exports are unencrypted and outside the vault’s protection. Decide how to protect that destination separately. Copying the app’s encrypted files alone is not a supported portable backup.</p>
<h2>If the phone still works</h2><ol><li>Keep the existing installation and device security intact.</li><li>If you can unlock the vault, export important files to a trusted destination.</li><li>Open the exported files and verify they are usable before changing phones or removing anything.</li><li>On another phone, import those copies into a newly configured vault, then review the intermediate copies.</li></ol>
<h2>If a file will not open</h2><p>Do not clear app data, reinstall or delete the item to troubleshoot. A failed preview may reflect an unsupported format. Try exporting the original. If integrity verification fails, preserve the stored file and contact support with a description of the error; do not send private photos or access secrets. Support cannot bypass encryption or reconstruct missing keys.</p>
<p>This limitation is part of the current product, not a claim that all vault apps behave this way. When comparing apps, check whether backup exists, whether it is encrypted and whether recovery has been tested on a different device.</p>''')]

def links():
    return '<ul>'+''.join(f'<li><a href="/private-eyes/guides/{slug}/">{escape(title)}</a></li>' for slug,title,_,_ in GUIDES)+'</ul>'

def product_extra():
    return '''<section><h2>Private files, kept on your phone</h2><div class="catalog-grid"><article class="app-card"><h3>No account or Internet permission</h3><p>Your vault works offline. The Android app has no analytics, advertising or cloud service.</p></article><article class="app-card"><h3>Protect the file and its details</h3><p>AES-256-GCM protects file contents, filenames and stored thumbnails. Android device authentication and your pattern control access.</p></article><article class="app-card"><h3>Capture without a Gallery original</h3><p>Take photos and short silent videos directly into the vault. Imports preserve original quality; export is an explicit action.</p></article></div></section>
<section><h2>From import to locked vault</h2><ol><li>Unlock and import selected photos, videos or documents.</li><li>Review successful imports and retry unfinished files.</li><li>Optionally choose Remove originals… and review the source provider or Android confirmation.</li><li>Lock the vault. Originals and exports elsewhere remain outside its protection.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>Know the limits before you start</h2><p>Android 13 or newer is required. Imports support up to 1 GiB per file. Camera video is silent and limited to two minutes / 120 MiB, subject to available memory. RAW originals can be stored; preview support varies.</p><p>There is no backup, recovery service or pattern reset. Losing the phone, its keys or your pattern can mean permanent loss. Do not keep the only copy of irreplaceable files here. Private Eyes is not independently security-certified and does not promise forensic invisibility.</p></section>
<section><h2>Android photo privacy guides</h2>'''+links()+'''</section><section><h2>Common questions</h2><h3>Does importing delete the original?</h3><p>No. Removal is optional after a successful import and depends on the source provider. Other copies and backups may remain.</p><h3>Can I use it without an account?</h3><p>Yes. The app works offline and has no sign-up or Internet permission.</p><h3>Can I recover files on a replacement phone?</h3><p>There is currently no portable encrypted backup or recovery service. Read the recovery guide before relying on local-only storage.</p><h3>Are exports encrypted?</h3><p>Exported copies are readable outside the vault. Choose their destination carefully.</p></section>'''

def build_guides(page):
    page('/private-eyes/guides/', 'Android Photo Privacy Guides', 'Practical guides to encrypted photo storage, removing originals, private capture and recovery limits on Android.', '<section class="catalog-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Private Eyes guides</p><h1>Keep your photo privacy choices clear</h1><p>Practical steps, current product limits and explanations of what happens to your files.</p></section>'+links()+'<p><a href="/private-eyes/">Explore Private Eyes for Android</a></p>')
    for slug,title,description,content in GUIDES:
        body='<article class="privacy-guide"><p><a href="/private-eyes/">Private Eyes</a> · <a href="/private-eyes/guides/">All photo privacy guides</a></p><h1>'+escape(title)+'</h1><p class="lead">'+escape(description)+'</p><p>Reviewed October 26, 2026 · TripperDeeLabs</p>'+content+'<section><h2>Related guides</h2>'+links()+'</section><p><a class="play-link" href="/private-eyes/">See Private Eyes features and Google Play download</a></p></article>'
        page('/private-eyes/guides/'+slug+'/', title, description, body)
